.TH EVENT_LOOP_DESTROY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
event_loop_destroy \- destroy an event_loop and invalidate the handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

event_loop_destroy(loop)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Macro wrapper around __event_loop_destroy. Blocks until every
in-flight resolved use of the handle has finished, shuts every
reactor thread down (if not already shut down), frees every
remaining registration, then sets loop to EVENT_LOOP_INVALID.

loop must be a currently live handle or EVENT_LOOP_INVALID (a
silent no-op). Calling this on a stale handle (one already
destroyed, sequentially by an earlier call or concurrently by
another thread) is a FATAL ERROR (abort()/SIGABRT), not a silent
double-free; see __event_loop_destroy(3) for further detail.

Calling this on loop from within a callback currently dispatching
on one of loop's own reactor/dispatch threads is likewise a FATAL
ERROR (abort()/SIGABRT), not a deadlock or a silent no-op; defer
destruction to another thread, or to after the callback returns,
instead.

See __event_loop_destroy(3) for the queue-outlives-the-loop safety
guarantee this provides for queue-backed registrations.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  An lvalue of type event_loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR __event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR ___event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR event_loop_create (3),
.BR event_loop_shutdown (3)
